#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"}] :question/id #uuid "ebb8f782-2616-52d1-8e6a-6c7049f9f9f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21890" :question/problem "![](images/64/649a710caaad131ccf79589e91d6059d8be55f2eb86230be6d3b44313a5c733d.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit shown in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance (in Farads).*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\cos(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $18\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac1{48}\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $6\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 18I = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/48)} = 48q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 6\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9\\cos(3t) = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 48q + 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $6,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = \\dfrac32\\cos(3t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "8ac6f533-446a-5d8c-863d-eb220e3dca77" :question/math-academy-id "e-21891" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitance of $\\dfrac{1}{20}\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductance of $L=10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is given below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f7e02ba534c41bec3783c56a5ce1b94422757f4398af0098db5ba9a0d6ec49e.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/20)} = 20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10 = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 1.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t \\to \\infty.$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n(0)+ 3(0) + 2q = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n2q = 1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\dfrac12$ coulombs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119548 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "f5902e80-c98b-599c-bd43-80075e7cc53c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21892" :question/problem "![](images/94/94d6100570bdcf98de739edfa18e68e5ba6889ce0ba7cd573cdb07c2cad68b33.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.05} = 20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(4t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 2\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+3(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) &  \\\\ \\,+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+(-12\\alpha -14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -14\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{6}{85}$ and $\\beta = -\\dfrac{7}{85}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t} - \\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{6}{85}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\dfrac{7}{85}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.11,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Current"}] :question/id #uuid "c2070894-8653-56e6-af9b-c683b053adc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21893" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t) = 4e^{-3t} - 2e^{-t} + \\dfrac5{17}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac3{17}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-t}+\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+2{e}^{-t}-\\frac{10}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{6}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = -12e^{-3t} + 2e^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{10}{17}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{6}{17}\\right)^2}\n= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{136}{289}}\n= \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{34}}{17}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce92f6a-8fea-5eac-b56f-9603bf1deecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{10}q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315195} {:answer/id #uuid "41adaad0-808a-5d11-80bf-27cce9437885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315499} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0a3fd6-c898-596c-bc1f-d0b2658d982e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315997} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6961e0-5841-5893-bb5c-73af9c32f184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316096} {:answer/id #uuid "b91ea2f3-94e8-5b89-978c-eede21ec744a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b91ea2f3-94e8-5b89-978c-eede21ec744a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316166} :answer-field/id #uuid "c01e3cd8-1fa9-5065-942d-77c41ea7a522" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22c646ac-7d01-5a7e-9969-fc276ffea8c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343714" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/16/169844e6d45393119ac1579cc89e34850401810bfcc1c7537d5b72851a0b2b02.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=20\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Current"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a369d386-1789-55a9-90f7-852ad4fcce9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118125}] :question/id #uuid "a7545e66-503e-5c9b-bd20-0b973a3cc598" :question/math-academy-id "q-343826" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n$q(t) = 3e^{-2t} - e^{-5t} + \\frac{7}{13}$.\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t) =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014cd28c-46c5-5b17-af0c-2dfd453b3b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "014cd28c-46c5-5b17-af0c-2dfd453b3b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315123} :answer-field/id #uuid "b08de74b-9df0-5ab8-a33a-ab423ff0e517" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0e3062a-d545-5008-96ee-4ac928ce7543" :question/math-academy-id "q-345015" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b885a0dc4bb868284ec96bcbfc964c767fc159aa0728044d722f0f6b11cbfb1f.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $14\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=14\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=14I=14\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+14\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=8.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+14(0)+10q=8,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=8\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{8}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{4}{5}$ coulombs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Current"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466d07aa-1ec9-5db3-a792-07caf8fef7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{-4t}-5{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "466d07aa-1ec9-5db3-a792-07caf8fef7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{-4t}-5{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317795} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a8a57c8-14d4-5721-92e8-993aec1910ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a15ce12f-67af-595a-8feb-031dd1000329" :question/math-academy-id "q-345088" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{-t}+8{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Current"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8b1a094-335a-586d-8c3d-03c43fed7e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8b1a094-335a-586d-8c3d-03c43fed7e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318224} :answer-field/id #uuid "26fca3f8-096e-5758-9d4d-a36cc9cf460a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e95a4d25-6c9d-57b1-8d08-3197c0c476a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345094" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)={e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{36+64}{25}}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083e53c2-e293-5e95-afb7-f240120d0b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317598} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2a45df-02e7-5ddb-ba89-f493b05e3245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318103} {:answer/id #uuid "adbca0b4-9d48-5b6f-b01e-f211f322c0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318155} {:answer/id #uuid "ee994e39-93a9-5c06-9e92-41fe7aeed852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318363} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8e2ad9-86b3-5ff9-ad70-e36660a30bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "083e53c2-e293-5e95-afb7-f240120d0b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317598} :answer-field/id #uuid "e53343f7-0df7-53e3-b136-454227114d26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50bfaf9e-5fa3-5ce2-93c0-cd914192855f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345115" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cc2afe50a1f1e3ed0a173cdba6f5a5f59145b0afa6038bee7c9a1fe720238a6.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Current"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57be75d5-8240-5b01-9208-f359500dbe5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57be75d5-8240-5b01-9208-f359500dbe5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317859} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e443742-3007-547f-91b1-6dc76f4fb385" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9538c7cd-06c1-5575-86de-72f831f368ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-345120" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363594f1-2121-5f25-94b7-d50c7b617cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "363594f1-2121-5f25-94b7-d50c7b617cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317737} :answer-field/id #uuid "68404b11-46c5-5be1-b877-8a693d48622b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e4d4641-9b55-5517-bac3-9fc3e587385e" :question/math-academy-id "q-345130" :question/problem "![](images/50/50bb98a563a0ee061a0faa4d4390aaf459a2fdfccb4721d5d7e6ddd3102c98b1.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I=12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=6.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+12(0)+4q=6,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n4q=6\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d60619b-e76d-5790-8aba-39d218afed81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317943} {:answer/id #uuid "732fdcb2-869e-554f-8854-7ab6543baf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317961} {:answer/id #uuid "83c93c03-7f9a-5a5f-ab3c-d9ff69e42544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318012} {:answer/id #uuid "8de5b245-4705-5de6-bf30-f81a91441578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318046} {:answer/id #uuid "d48830f4-0bc6-54ba-a8e9-bc11390ebdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de5b245-4705-5de6-bf30-f81a91441578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318046} :answer-field/id #uuid "25fb76b0-9b9a-5e94-af61-d4768b49b60d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f781abc5-81a3-5e42-a46f-9bd991ddcdd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345135" :question/problem "![](images/11/1146917884634beea990c281a591257898887a11029f0116e10f8bfd38169282.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+3(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{7}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{7}{10})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}≈0.71,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006fab69-d6f3-56c8-8155-a3e19388f4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317571} {:answer/id #uuid "3e841afd-07d0-5f8c-8863-bb24e07697a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317764} {:answer/id #uuid "64ff1ba2-fd7e-5c02-ba32-921ff68c9177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317912} {:answer/id #uuid "981fa6db-af4b-5154-8495-3b9f55facab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318084} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b42a27-633b-52a5-ba1c-e5e20732caf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64ff1ba2-fd7e-5c02-ba32-921ff68c9177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317912} :answer-field/id #uuid "4665862c-886b-5080-9686-2600e000dfbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c27e0ae-78a1-5371-b073-4bc4c4aae1d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345144" :question/problem "![](images/42/42ab81638355355340008eaa54657a0144612ec9d04e44266350cdb8012051de.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-18\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +18\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -5\\beta =-\\frac{41}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}≈0.45,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
